<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mátyás Rákosi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1892–1971</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mátyás Rákosi was the leader of the Hungarian communists who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Using the police and 'salami tactics' to break up rival parties, he established the Hungarian People's Republic in 1949 and ruled as a Stalinist dictator, with show trials and mass repression. He was removed in July 1956, months before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848">Hungarian Revolution</a>, and died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi.jpg" alt="Mátyás Rákosi" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%A1ty%C3%A1s_R%C3%A1kosi_General_Secretary_of_the_Hungarian_Communist_Party_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
